WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.280
Lawyers, regulators, and hospital
administrators face immense pressure.

2
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:09.600
You must integrate generative AI into your
operations without exposing

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.960
your organization to legal or reputational
ruin.

4
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:16.960
When you evaluate these systems, vendors
present pitches loaded with

5
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.840
assurances. They claim their models are
99% safe and rigorously

6
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:23.680
aligned with human values.

7
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.600
These claims rely almost entirely on
behavioral safety.

8
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.320
Engineers train the AI to
probabilistically favor polite, harmless outputs

9
00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:33.840
over malicious ones.

10
00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.520
But a statistical propensity to follow
instructions is not a

11
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.120
guarantee. Treating it as one is an
exercise in compliance

12
00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.800
theater, generating a feeling of security
that the underlying code

13
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.720
cannot support. High-stakes environments
require structural, deterministic bounds, absolute

14
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.280
limits on what a system is permitted to
do.

15
00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.040
Probabilistic models only offer a
likelihood of good behavior.

16
00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.680
To understand what actual safety looks
like, we have to

17
00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.640
look past the marketing.

18
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.520
We will examine the math vendors use to
inflate their

19
00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.160
safety scores and detail the architectural
security required to genuinely

20
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:10.680
protect an enterprise.

21
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:15.240
For professionals holding legal liability,
a vendor's probabilistic promise is

22
00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.680
an unacceptable substitute for verifiable
engineering.

23
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.240
Safety reports consistently feature one
specific number, the attack success

24
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.000
rate, or ASR.

25
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.800
Vendors use this metric to claim an
incredibly low rate

26
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.080
of model failure.

27
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.440
To calculate ASR at an industrial scale,
vendors do not

28
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.960
check outputs by hand.

29
00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.040
They use automated keyword classifiers
that scan the AI's responses

30
00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.120
for standard refusal phrases, like I
cannot fulfill this request.

31
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:47.000
This confusion matrix plots automated
classifiers against human-verified ground truth.

32
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:51.080
They produce an 88% false positive rate,
flagging harmless errors

33
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.480
because the output lacked descripted
refusal.

34
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.920
Correcting this drops the 36%..2 ASR to
159.

35
00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.000
Cohen's Kappa measures this agreement at a
dismal 0.245.

36
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.320
This mathematical inflation allows vendors
to market a highly resilient

37
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:09.840
model. The numbers look excellent on
paper, but they reflect

38
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.200
the strictness of a keyword search, not
the actual security

39
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.320
of the system.

40
00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.160
Relying on headline attack success rates
without auditing the underlying

41
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.480
classifier methodology leaves decision
makers blind to how brittle the

42
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.680
system actually is.

43
00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:26.960
Vendors also base their safety claims on
testing thousands of

44
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.720
harmful prompts. If those prompts are
simple, predictable variations of

45
00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.680
known bad inputs, the model passes easily.

46
00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:37.280
This line graph shows reasoning
capabilities on the x-axis and

47
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.360
vulnerability on the y-axis.

48
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.880
While a model blocks direct queries about
explosives, phrasing the

49
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:47.440
request as adversarial poetry bypasses
Frontier models 62% of the

50
00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.000
time. Real risk lives in this long tail..

51
00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:54.680
This reveals the reasoning penalty Highly
capable, advanced autols are

52
00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.800
paradoxically less safe than their simpler
predecessors.

53
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:01.720
The same advanced reasoning capabilities
that allow an AI to

54
00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.720
follow complex logic also allow it to
convince itself that

55
00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.240
a malicious request is actually a benign
component of a

56
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:14.600
larger task. Static, list-based safeguards
provide zero protection against this.

57
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:18.520
The AI's own intelligence becomes the
exact mechanism used to

58
00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.760
bypass the behavioral constraints.

59
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.880
We have to define the boundaries of
behavioral safety.

60
00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.400
This encompasses any training mechanism
that relies on the model's

61
00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.680
internal statistical weights to dictate
its final output.

62
00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.120
Trusting behavioral safety is the
equivalent of asking a driver

63
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.720
to promise they won't speed.

64
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:40.040
The alignment is brittle, probabilistic,
and easily overwritten by rearranging

65
00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.280
the prompt. Enterprise risk mitigation
requires architectural safety.

66
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.000
This means placing the probabilistic AI
model inside an enclosure

67
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.160
of rigid deterministic code.

68
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.920
A true system includes distinct non-AI
components, independent monitors, hard

69
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.080
veto gates, and action classifiers
observing from the outside.

70
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:04.240
These external systems operate on strict
deterministic logic.

71
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.720
They block harmful actions entirely,
regardless of what the probabilistic

72
00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:11.360
AI model internally decided to do.

73
00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:15.200
To return to our driver analogy,
architectural safety is the

74
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.080
installation of a physical mechanical
governor.

75
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:22.320
Speeding becomes physically impossible, no
matter the driver's intent.

76
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.920
In high-stakes fields, safety is not about
training a system

77
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:27.360
to want to be good.

78
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.320
It is the practice of engineering a system
where unsafe

79
00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.800
actions are structurally impossible.

80
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:35.920
The industry is transitioning out of the
experimental phase of

81
00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.000
AI adoption and directly into the phase of
professional liability.

82
00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:43.480
The novelty of the technology is no longer
a defense.

83
00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.560
Non-technical decision makers can enforce
strict due diligence by applying

84
00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:51.400
the five-question architectural test to
every vendor pitch.

85
00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:54.120
You must demand public artifacts.

86
00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.040
Look at the vendor's architecture diagram
and verify the existence

87
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:02.520
of independent safety boxes that sit
entirely outside the AI

88
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:05.720
model. Review the liability attestation.

89
00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:09.800
If a hallucinated output generates a
flawed legal citation or

90
00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:14.200
a dangerous medical protocol, the
professional operating the system bears

91
00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:18.120
the reputational and legal cost, not the
software vendor.

92
00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:21.240
If a vendor presents an architecture
diagram that consists of

93
00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.840
a single box labeled AI model, they do not
possess

94
00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.360
a safety architecture.

95
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:28.520
They are selling a naked model.

96
00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.920
Professional literacy is your only defense
against compliance theater.

97
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:37.760
In an adversarial environment, a
well-behaved AI is never enough.

98
00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:39.920
True safety must be audited and verified.

